---
/**
 * The privacy policy text, shared by the live page and /next/. Edit
 * the wording here; the "last updated" date lives in lib/legal.ts.
 *
 * DRAFT (October 8, 2026 update) for Derek and counsel to review before it
 * ships. Each statement about what the app does was checked against the app
 * repo; `REVIEW:` comments mark commitments, and things the code could not
 * confirm.
 */
const ink = "text-[var(--color-ink)]";
const soft = "mt-3 text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]";
const list = "mt-3 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]";
const link =
  "underline decoration-[var(--color-rule)] underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-[var(--color-ink)]";
---

<div class="prose-letter space-y-8">
  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Overview</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      Harvous is a Bible study notes app operated by Testament Made LLC ("we," "us"). This policy
      explains what information we collect, how we use it, who else is involved, and the choices
      you have. A plain-English list of what changed in this version is on the
      <a class={link} href="/legal/changes/">What changed</a> page.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Information we collect</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Account information.</strong> When you sign up, we use Clerk for
      authentication. Clerk may collect your email address, name, profile photo, and the sign-in
      details needed to verify your identity. We keep a copy of your name, email, and profile
      photo with your account so the app can show them.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Your content.</strong> We store the notes, threads, folders, spaces,
      highlights, connections, and related study data you create in Harvous, along with earlier
      versions of your notes. This content is tied to your account and is private by default.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Your activity in the app.</strong> To make Harvous useful when you come
      back to it, we keep a record of some of what you do in it:
    </p>
    <ul class={list}>
      <li>
        Which Bible chapters you open, in which translation, and roughly how long you stayed (a
        glance, a read, or a longer study).
      </li>
      <li>Which notes you open, and roughly how long you stayed.</li>
      <li>
        What you search for, how many results came back, and whether you opened one. The words you
        search are stored on our servers so you can see recent searches and so Harvous can notice
        a question you keep asking. They are never sent to our analytics provider; it only learns
        how long a search was.
      </li>
      <li>How you respond to suggestions on Home, such as opening or snoozing one.</li>
      <li>
        If you use Review, what you've chosen to come back to, when it's next due, how you rated
        each answer, and anything you typed before revealing the answer.
      </li>
      <li>When you were last active, and your time zone.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class={soft}>
      We use this to run features for you: Home, where you left off, suggestions, Review, and
      reminders at a sensible hour. It is not used for advertising, it is not sold, and it is
      never shown to your church.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Usage and device data.</strong> We collect technical and product-usage
      information to keep Harvous reliable and to understand how the app is used — for example
      browser or device type, app version, pages or screens viewed, feature usage, and error
      reports. This may be associated with your account (such as your user id, and limited
      profile details like email or name) so we can improve the product.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Anonymous diagnostics.</strong> When something breaks, such as a script
      error or a failed request, the app sends us a short report so we can fix it. The report
      includes a cleaned-up error message, the kind of page you were on (with ids removed), your
      platform, app version, and browser family, and a random id that changes every week. It does
      not include your account id, name, email, or any note content, and it is not linked to your
      account. You can also send one yourself from Settings › Support.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Reminders and notifications.</strong> If you turn on reminders on a
      device, we store that device's push address, its browser type, and your reminder schedule.
      For each reminder we send, we record when it went out and what happened next — whether it
      was tapped or dismissed, or whether you opened Harvous within a few hours — so Harvous can
      send fewer reminders when they aren't helping. A reminder may mention a Bible passage you
      were reading.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Payments.</strong> If you buy Harvous Plus or another paid plan, Polar
      processes the payment as the seller of record. Polar handles your card details; we don't
      receive or store your full card number. We keep a record of your plan and whether it is
      active.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Support requests.</strong> When you contact us through the app, we store
      your message along with your name, email, app version, the page you were on, and basic
      details about your browser or device, so we can help.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Email and messaging.</strong> If you have an account or subscribe to
      updates, we may store your email and related preferences so we can send product or service
      communications (for example, onboarding or important account notices).
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">How we use your information</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      We use your information to provide and improve Harvous: syncing your notes across devices,
      authenticating your account, running features like Home, Review, and reminders, analyzing
      product usage, responding to support requests, processing payments, sending relevant
      product or service emails, and protecting the service from abuse. We do not sell your
      personal information.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Trying Harvous without an account</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      You can try Harvous before signing up. What you make as a guest, such as highlights and
      notes, is kept only in your browser on that device; it isn't sent to our servers. If you then
      create an account, Harvous copies it into your new account and clears the copy on the
      device. If you don't, it stays in your browser until you clear your browser's data.
    </p>
    {/* REVIEW: guest visits may still load product analytics (PostHog) before sign-up; that
         code path was not traced for guests. Confirm before publishing. */}
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Sharing and other people</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      Your notes are private unless you choose to share them. When you create a share link, only
      the content you explicitly share becomes accessible to people with that link.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Shared spaces.</strong> When you join or create a shared space, the
      other people in it can see what is added to that space, and your name and profile photo. The
      person who owns the space can also see your email address. Your notes outside the space stay
      private.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Churches.</strong> If you connect Harvous to your church, the church's
      staff see how many people are connected and how many follow each of its channels. They do
      not see what you read, write, search, or review. Staff can see who follows a church channel,
      so they can look after it and choose its leaders; other followers can't. If you send
      something to your church, such as a note for one of its channels or a suggested resource,
      the staff who review it see it and your name.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      To help you find your church, we search the Here's My Church directory. We send it what you
      type in the search box, and if you add a church that isn't listed, the church's name and
      address. We don't send it your name, email, or account details. To place a church you add on
      the map, we look up its address with OpenStreetMap's Nominatim service.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>Discover.</strong> Discover is a public catalog of study starters people
      give away. If you submit something, we review it before it is listed. Once listed, it is
      public in the app and on harvous.com, with your first name and last initial, and anyone can
      add a copy to their own Harvous account. You can withdraw it at any time; copies people
      already added stay in their accounts.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Locked notes</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      You can lock a note with a PIN. A locked note is encrypted in your browser with a key made
      from your PIN before it is saved, and the key never leaves your device. We store only the
      encrypted text, so we cannot read a locked note and cannot recover it if you forget your PIN.
      To check your PIN when you set it, change it, or lock a note, the app sends it to us over an
      encrypted connection; we keep only a salted hash of it, never the PIN itself. Exports
      include a placeholder in place of each locked note's content.
    </p>
    {/* REVIEW: the previous policy said "your PIN is never sent to us". That was not accurate:
         POST /api/user/set-lock-pin and /verify-lock-pin receive the PIN and store a salted hash
         (src/utils/lock-pin-server.ts). docs/LOCKED_NOTES_ENCRYPTION.md makes the same claim. A
         4-digit PIN can also be guessed offline by anyone holding the encrypted text; consider
         saying so, or offering a longer passphrase. */}
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Connector and AI apps</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      If you use Connector, which is part of Harvous Plus, you can connect AI apps such as Claude or
      ChatGPT to your account. A connected app can read your notes, threads, highlights,
      connections, the spaces you belong to, and the Bible chapters you've read recently, when you
      ask it questions. It cannot change or delete anything. If you turn on "Let apps start notes"
      (it's off until you do), an app can also start a new note for you, with its summary of your
      conversation shown on a card in that note. Locked notes are not readable, and Bible verse text
      is not sent, only references. What an app does with what it reads is governed by that
      service's own terms and privacy policy, not this one. Harvous records which apps you have
      connected, when each last used the connection, and how many requests were made and notes
      started each day. You can see connected apps and disconnect any of them in Settings ›
      Connector.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Service providers</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      We use trusted service providers who process data on our behalf under appropriate
      agreements. Each only receives what it needs to do its job:
    </p>
    <ul class={list}>
      <li><strong class={ink}>Clerk</strong> — sign-in and accounts.</li>
      <li>
        <strong class={ink}>Supabase</strong> — our database and file storage, including daily
        backups.
      </li>
      <li><strong class={ink}>Fly.io</strong> — runs the servers behind the app.</li>
      <li>
        <strong class={ink}>Cloudflare</strong> — delivers the app and this website, and helps
        protect them from abuse.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class={ink}>PostHog</strong> — product analytics: pages viewed and features used,
        with your user id, name, and email.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class={ink}>Audienceful</strong> — email. It receives your email, name, account
        id, when you signed up, last signed in, and were last active, and simple yes-or-no flags
        such as whether you've created a note or shared something. Never what you wrote.
      </li>
      <li><strong class={ink}>Polar</strong> — payments, as the seller of record.</li>
      <li>
        <strong class={ink}>Slack</strong> — alerts us to new support requests, with the request
        number, topic, your name or email, and the first line of your message.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class={ink}>Your browser's push service</strong> (for example Apple, Google, or
        Mozilla) — delivers reminders to devices where you turned them on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class={ink}>Here's My Church</strong> and <strong class={ink}>OpenStreetMap
        Nominatim</strong> — the church directory and address lookup described above. Neither
        receives your account details.
      </li>
    </ul>
    {/* REVIEW: netlify.toml is still in the app repo and Netlify is still a paid account.
         Production was cut over to Cloudflare + Fly; confirm Netlify no longer handles any user
         traffic before leaving it off this list. Also decide whether to say where data is
         stored (the Fly region is iad, US; the Supabase region was not checked). */}
    <p class={soft}>
      We configure analytics with privacy in mind: we do not enable session recording, we do not
      turn on broad automatic click capture, and we respect browser Do Not Track settings when
      they are enabled.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Who at Harvous can see your data</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      Harvous is run by a very small team. We read the support requests you send us. We have
      technical access to the systems that store your account, which we need to keep Harvous
      running, but we don't browse your notes. We look at your content only when you ask us to
      help with something that needs it, or when we must to keep the service secure or to follow
      the law. We can never read a locked note.
    </p>
    {/* REVIEW: this paragraph is a commitment about practice, not something the code enforces
         (the app has no in-app admin view of a user's notes, but the database is reachable).
         Confirm it describes how Derek actually works. */}
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Cookies</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      Harvous uses cookies and similar technologies (including local storage) to keep you signed
      in, remember basic preferences, and support product analytics. You can control cookies
      through your browser settings, though some features may not work without them. Enabling Do
      Not Track in your browser may limit analytics collection.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Your choices</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      In Settings › My Notes you can download your notes at any time, as a full backup (.zip),
      Markdown, or a spreadsheet (CSV); clear your search history; clear all your notes while
      keeping your account; or delete your account. You can turn reminders off in Settings, and
      disconnect AI apps in Settings › Connector.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Data retention and deletion</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      We retain your account and content for as long as your account is active. Search history
      you haven't cleared is kept, but Harvous only uses the last 90 days of it.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      <strong class={ink}>When you delete your account,</strong> we delete what your account
      holds: your notes and everything attached to them, your highlights, threads, folders, and
      tags, the spaces you own and your place in other people's, your reading and search history,
      Review, reminders and the devices they go to, Connector access, Discover listings, support
      requests, imports, templates, your profile and settings, and your sign-in account with
      Clerk. Any active subscription is cancelled at once.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      We also delete the copies our providers hold: your email subscription (Audienceful), your
      analytics profile (PostHog), and your billing record with Polar, which is anonymized — Polar
      keeps payment records it must keep for tax and legal reasons. Your daily backup files are
      deleted too. Copies of something you listed on Discover that other people already added to
      their own accounts stay theirs.
    </p>
    <p class={soft}>
      If you helped run a church in Harvous, the church's own records — its services, series, and
      ministries — stay with the church. Anonymous diagnostics are not linked to your account, so
      they are not affected. Some information may also be kept where required for legal or
      security purposes.
    </p>
    {/* REVIEW: true once the app's account-deletion change ships (harvous repo commit 144532aa7,
         server/utils/delete-account.ts). PostHog deletion only runs when POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY
         and POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID are set on the server — set them before publishing this. */}
    {/* REVIEW: Supabase's own database backups (point-in-time recovery) were not checked; if the
         plan keeps them, add their window here. */}
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      When we make a meaningful change to this policy, we'll tell you in the app and by email, and
      summarize what changed on the <a class={link} href="/legal/changes/">What changed</a> page.
      The update is effective on the date shown at the top. If you don't agree with it, you can
      stop using Harvous and delete your account.
    </p>
    {/* REVIEW: the "by email" commitment needs an actual email send for each update. */}
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Contact</h2>
    <p class={soft}>
      Questions about this policy or your data? Email
      <a class={link} href="mailto:derek@harvous.com">
        derek@harvous.com
      </a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
